// ─── CSS Interview Questions (200) ──────────────────────────────────────
export const CSS_QUESTIONS = [
  // ── Easy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is CSS and what does it stand for?", "easy", "CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. It's a stylesheet language used to describe the presentation of HTML documents — controlling layout, colors, fonts, spacing, animations, and responsive behavior. CSS separates content (HTML) from presentation, enabling maintainable, consistent styling across pages."],
  ["What are the three ways to apply CSS to an HTML document?", "easy", "Inline: style attribute on an HTML element (<div style='color:red'>). Internal: <style> tag inside the document <head>. External: separate .css file linked via <link rel='stylesheet' href='styles.css'>. External is best practice — cached by browser, reusable across pages, keeps HTML clean."],
  ["What is the CSS box model?", "easy", "The box model describes how every HTML element is rendered as a rectangular box. From inside out: content → padding → border → margin. Total width = content + padding + border + margin (with content-box). With border-box, width includes content + padding + border (margin still extra). The box model determines element sizing, spacing, and layout behavior."],
  ["What is the difference between margin and padding?", "easy", "Padding is the space inside the element, between content and border (part of the element's background area). Margin is the space outside the element, between the border and adjacent elements (transparent, collapses). Padding adds to element size (content-box) unless box-sizing: border-box. Margins collapse vertically between block elements."],
  ["What is the difference between border-box and content-box?", "easy", "content-box (default): width/height set only content area. padding and border add to total size (element renders larger than specified). border-box: width/height include content + padding + border. Padding and border shrink content area instead of expanding element. border-box makes sizing more intuitive — a 100px wide element stays 100px regardless of padding/border."],
  ["What are CSS selectors? Name the five types.", "easy", "Universal (*), type/element (div, p), class (.class), id (#id), attribute ([type='text']), pseudo-class (:hover), pseudo-element (::before), combinator (div > p, div + p, div ~ p), grouping (div, p, .class). These selectors target HTML elements to apply styles."],
  ["What is specificity in CSS and how is it calculated?", "easy", "Specificity determines which CSS rule applies when multiple rules target the same element. Calculated as a 4-part score: inline styles (1,0,0,0), IDs (0,1,0,0), classes/attributes/pseudo-classes (0,0,1,0), elements/pseudo-elements (0,0,0,1). Higher score wins. !important overrides everything (use sparingly). Tie-breaking: later rule wins if specificity equal."],
  ["What does !important do and why should you avoid it?", "easy", "!important overrides all specificity rules — the property is applied regardless of selector specificity. Avoid because: breaks natural cascade, makes debugging difficult, overrides can only be undone by another !important (specificity arms race), makes code harder to maintain. Use specific selectors or increase specificity instead of !important."],
  ["What is the difference between display: none and visibility: hidden?", "easy", "display: none removes the element from the document flow (no space occupied, not rendered, not accessible). visibility: hidden hides the element visually but it still takes up space in the layout and is in the accessibility tree (just invisible). display: none affects layout; visibility: hidden does not. Use display: none to truly remove; visibility: hidden to hide while preserving space."],
  ["What is the difference between display: none and opacity: 0?", "easy", "display: none removes element from flow (no space, not interactive). opacity: 0 makes element transparent but it remains in flow (still takes space, still interactive — can receive clicks, still in accessibility tree). opacity: 0 also renders a stacking context. Use opacity: 0 for invisible but interactive elements; display: none for elements that should not exist in layout."],
  ["What is the difference between block, inline, and inline-block?", "easy", "block: takes full width, starts new line, respects width/height/margin/padding. inline: flows inline (no new line), ignores width/height, padding/margin only horizontal. inline-block: inline flow but block-like sizing — respects width/height, padding/margin all sides, doesn't start new line. inline-block is useful for grid-like layouts without flexbox."],
  ["What is the difference between relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky positioning?", "easy", "static: default, in normal flow. relative: positioned relative to its normal position (top/left offset from where it would be). absolute: removed from flow, positioned relative to nearest positioned ancestor (or initial containing block). fixed: removed from flow, positioned relative to viewport (stays on scroll). sticky: hybrid of relative + fixed — behaves as relative until scroll threshold, then fixed. Only positioned elements (not static) can use z-index and be absolute ancestors."],
  ["What is the z-index property and when does it work?", "easy", "z-index controls stacking order of overlapping elements. Higher value = closer to viewer. Only works on positioned elements (position: relative/absolute/fixed/sticky) and flex/grid items. Default is auto (stack in order of DOM). Negative z-index goes behind parent. z-index creates a stacking context — elements in a higher stacking context appear above lower ones regardless of z-index values."],
  ["What is the difference between em and rem units?", "easy", "em is relative to the parent element's font-size. rem (root em) is relative to the root element's font-size (<html>, default 16px). em cascades — nested elements compound (1.5em inside 1.5em parent = 2.25em of root). rem stays consistent regardless of nesting. Use rem for consistent sizing across the page; em for component-relative sizing (buttons, headings)."],
  ["What are vw and vh units?", "easy", "vw (viewport width): 1vw = 1% of viewport width. vh (viewport height): 1vh = 1% of viewport height. 100vw = full viewport width (may overflow due to scrollbar). 100vh = full viewport height (mobile browsers have address bar issues — use 100dvh for dynamic viewport). Related: vmin = min(vw, vh), vmax = max(vw, vh). Useful for full-screen layouts, hero sections, responsive typography."],
  ["What is a CSS pseudo-class? Give five examples.", "easy", "Pseudo-classes select elements based on state, not attributes. Examples: :hover (mouse over), :focus (keyboard/click focus), :nth-child(2) (second child), :disabled (disabled form elements), :first-child (first child of parent). They're prefixed with single colon. Used for interactive states, structural targeting, and form validation states."],
  ["What is a CSS pseudo-element? Give three examples.", "easy", "Pseudo-elements target specific parts of an element. Prefixed with double colons (::). Examples: ::before (inserts content before element), ::after (inserts content after), ::first-letter (first letter of text), ::first-line (first line), ::placeholder (input placeholder text), ::selection (user-selected text). ::before/::after require content property to render."],
  ["What is the difference between :hover and :focus?", "easy", ":hover applies when the user hovers with a pointing device (mouse). :focus applies when the element receives focus (click, tab key, keyboard navigation). They overlap on mouse click (both hover and focus apply). For accessibility: always provide :focus styles for keyboard users, not just :hover. :focus-visible is preferred for focus rings (only shows for keyboard users, not mouse clicks)."],
  ["What are CSS custom properties (variables)?", "easy", "CSS custom properties (--name) define reusable values that can be referenced with var(). Defined on elements (often :root for global). Inherit through the cascade — can be overridden per scope. Dynamic — can be changed at runtime via JavaScript or media queries (unlike Sass variables). Example: :root { --primary: blue; } .btn { background: var(--primary); }. Enable theme switching, design tokens, and reduced repetition."],
  ["How do you define and use a CSS variable?", "easy", "Define: --variable-name: value; (usually on :root). Use: property: var(--variable-name, fallback). Example: :root { --primary-color: #007bff; --spacing: 1rem; }. .btn { background: var(--primary-color); margin: var(--spacing); }. Fallback value used if variable not defined. Variables inherit (children can override parent values). Override in specific scopes for theming."],
  ["What does the var() function do in CSS?", "easy", "var(--name, fallback) retrieves the value of a custom property (CSS variable). First argument is the variable name (required). Second is an optional fallback used if the variable is not defined. var() works in any property value, including shorthand values. Example: font: var(--font-size) var(--font-family). Fallbacks can be comma-separated values: background: var(--bg, linear-gradient(black, white))."],
  ["What are CSS transitions?", "easy", "CSS transitions animate changes between two property states. Defined by: transition-property (which properties to animate), transition-duration (animation length), transition-timing-function (ease, linear, ease-in-out, cubic-bezier), transition-delay (wait before starting). Shorthand: transition: all 0.3s ease. Transitions require a trigger (hover, class change, etc.). Best for simple state changes; use animations for complex sequences."],
  ["What are CSS animations and @keyframes?", "easy", "CSS animations run named sequences defined with @keyframes. Keyframes define property values at percentages (0%-100% or from/to). animation properties: animation-name, animation-duration, animation-timing-function, animation-iteration-count (infinite), animation-direction (normal, reverse, alternate), animation-fill-mode (forwards, backwards, both). Animations run automatically on load or on class addition. More powerful than transitions for multi-step, looping, or auto-starting effects."],
  ["What is the difference between a transition and an animation?", "easy", "Transitions: move between two states (start → end), require a trigger (hover, class change), single-step (0% to 100%), limited control. Animations: multi-keyframe sequences, can auto-start, loop infinitely, run reverse/alternate, offer full control at each step. Use transitions for simple hover/state changes; animations for loading spinners, entrance effects, complex sequences."],
  ["What is flexbox?", "easy", "Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) is a 1D layout model for distributing space and aligning items in a container. display: flex on parent enables flex layout. Main axis (flex-direction: row/column) and cross axis (perpendicular). Items stretch, shrink, and align along both axes. Key properties: justify-content (main axis), align-items (cross axis), flex-wrap, gap. Flexbox excels at component-level layouts, centering, navigation bars, and responsive alignment."],
  ["What is the default flex-direction?", "easy", "The default flex-direction is row (left to right in LTR languages). Items are placed horizontally along the main axis. Changing to column stacks items vertically. flex-direction: row-reverse or column-reverse reverses the start/end positions. Default flex-wrap is nowrap (items shrink to fit unless flex-shrink disabled)."],
  ["What does justify-content do in flexbox?", "easy", "justify-content aligns items along the main axis (horizontal for flex-direction: row). Values: flex-start (default, items at start), flex-end (items at end), center (centered), space-between (equal space between items, none at edges), space-around (equal space around each item), space-evenly (equal space between all items and edges). Space distribution only applies when items don't fill the container."],
  ["What does align-items do in flexbox?", "easy", "align-items aligns items along the cross axis (perpendicular to main axis). Values: stretch (default, items fill container height), flex-start (items top/left), flex-end (items bottom/right), center (vertically centered), baseline (aligned to text baseline). align-items affects ALL items; align-self overrides for individual items."],
  ["What is CSS Grid?", "easy", "CSS Grid is a 2D layout system with rows AND columns. display: grid on parent enables grid layout. Define columns with grid-template-columns, rows with grid-template-rows. Gap (grid-gap) for spacing. Place items: grid-column, grid-row, or grid-area for named areas. Explicit grid (defined tracks) vs implicit grid (auto-generated). Grid is ideal for page-level layouts, while flexbox is for 1D component layouts."],
  ["What does grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr) create?", "easy", "Creates 3 equal-width columns. repeat(3, 1fr) is shorthand for 1fr 1fr 1fr — three fraction units. Each column gets 1/3 of available space (after fixed-size content). 1fr = one fraction of remaining space. Good for responsive grids without media queries: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)) creates flexible columns."],

  // ── Medium ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is the CSS cascade and how does it determine which styles apply?", "med", "The cascade is the algorithm that resolves conflicting CSS declarations. Steps: 1) Importance & Origin (author !important > author normal > user agent), 2) Specificity (inline > id > class > element), 3) Source Order (later declarations override earlier ones at same specificity). The cascade also considers: origin (author, user, user-agent), layers (@layer), and scope. Understanding the cascade is essential for debugging CSS conflicts."],
  ["What is the difference between the cascade and inheritance?", "med", "Cascade resolves conflicts between rules targeting the same element. Inheritance propagates certain properties (color, font-family) from parent to child automatically. Not all properties inherit (width, margin, padding, background don't). Use inherit keyword to force inheritance, initial to reset to default, unset to either inherit or initial (depends on property), revert to browser default."],
  ["What is margin collapsing and when does it happen?", "med", "Margin collapsing: adjacent vertical margins combine (collapse) into the larger margin instead of adding. Happens between: adjacent siblings (top of A + bottom of B), parent and first/last child (child margin overflows through parent if no border/padding), empty blocks (both margins collapse). Only vertical margins (top/bottom) collapse — horizontal never collapse. Block formatting contexts, padding, border stop collapsing."],
  ["What is the difference between flex: 1 and width: 100%?", "med", "flex: 1 makes an item grow to fill remaining space in the flex container, considering other flex items. width: 100% sets the item to exactly 100% of the parent width, potentially overflowing if there are other items. flex: 1 is responsive to siblings; width: 100% is not. flex: 1 also allows shrinking; width: 100% may overflow. Use flex: 1 for flexible fill; width: 100% for exact sizing."],
  ["What is the difference between align-items and align-content in flexbox?", "med", "align-items aligns individual items along the cross axis (per-item alignment). align-content aligns multiple lines of items (only applies when flex-wrap: wrap and items fill multiple lines). align-content distributes space BETWEEN lines; align-items positions items within their line. align-content has no effect on single-line flex containers."],
  ["What is the difference between flex-grow, flex-shrink, and flex-basis?", "med", "flex-grow: how much an item grows relative to others when there's extra space (0 = no grow). flex-shrink: how much an item shrinks when space is tight (1 = default, can shrink). flex-basis: initial main-axis size before growing/shrinking (auto = content size, 200px = fixed). Shorthand: flex: grow shrink basis (e.g., flex: 1 0 200px = grow evenly, no shrink, start at 200px)."],
  ["How do you span a grid item across multiple columns?", "med", "Use grid-column: span N or grid-column: start / end. Examples: grid-column: span 2 (spans 2 columns), grid-column: 1 / 3 (columns 1 to 3), grid-column: 1 / -1 (all columns, -1 = last line). Same for rows: grid-row: span 2. Items can overlap using explicit start/end lines. Combined with grid-template-areas for named layouts: grid-area: header spans entire named area."],
  ["What is the difference between implicit and explicit CSS Grid tracks?", "med", "Explicit grid: tracks defined with grid-template-columns and grid-template-rows. Implicit grid: auto-generated tracks when items are placed outside the explicit grid. Sized by grid-auto-rows and grid-auto-columns (default auto). Implicit tracks may stretch content. Use grid-auto-rows: minmax(100px, auto) to control auto-generated row sizes. Items that overflow the explicit grid create implicit tracks."],
  ["What does minmax() do in CSS Grid?", "med", "minmax(min, max) sets a track size range. The track will be at least min and at most max. Example: grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(200px, 1fr)) — each column at least 200px but can grow equally. minmax(0, 1fr) allows tracks to shrink to 0 (vs default minmax(auto, 1fr) where content prevents shrinking below content size). Essential for responsive grids without media queries."],
  ["What is the difference between auto-fill and auto-fit in CSS Grid?", "med", "auto-fill: fills the row with as many tracks as possible, keeping empty tracks. auto-fit: fits available tracks, collapsing empty ones (their space distributed among non-empty tracks). With auto-fill, empty columns maintain width. With auto-fit, empty columns collapse to 0 width. Use auto-fill for consistent column sizes; auto-fit for content that should stretch to fill available space."],
  ["What is a stacking context and what creates one?", "med", "A stacking context is a 3D conceptual grouping of elements along the z-axis. Elements within a stacking context are stacked relative to each other. Created by: position with z-index (not auto), opacity < 1, transform, filter, isolation: isolate, contain: paint, mix-blend-mode, will-change, position: fixed/sticky. Each stacking context isolates its children from the external z-index order."],
  ["What is the difference between position: fixed and position: sticky?", "med", "fixed: element is removed from flow and positioned relative to the viewport (or nearest transformed ancestor). Stays in same place regardless of scroll. sticky: element behaves as relative until it crosses a scroll threshold (top: 0, bottom: 0), then becomes fixed relative to its parent container. Sticky only works within its parent (stops at parent boundary). Fixed is always viewport-relative; sticky is container-relative with scroll awareness."],
  ["What is the CSS calc() function?", "med", "calc() performs arithmetic in CSS property values. Supports +, -, *, /. Mix units: width: calc(100% - 20px), font-size: calc(1rem + 0.5vw). Spaces around operators required. calc() is evaluated at computed-value time (not parse time), enabling responsive math. Use for fluid layouts, minus padding, combining relative/absolute units."],
  ["What are CSS media queries?", "med", "Media queries apply styles based on device characteristics (viewport width, height, orientation, resolution, color scheme, etc.). Syntax: @media (condition) { /* styles */ }. Common: @media (min-width: 768px) { ... }, @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { ... }, @media (hover: hover) { ... }. Enable responsive design — different layouts per device. Combine conditions with and, comma (or), not."],
  ["What is the mobile-first approach in CSS?", "med", "Mobile-first: write base styles for small screens first, then add min-width media queries for larger screens. This means the mobile layout is the default (no media query needed). Larger layouts build on top with overrides. Benefits: smaller CSS (one base, additive overrides), progressive enhancement, better performance on mobile (no query parsing overhead). Avoid max-width queries (desktop-first)."],
  ["What are breakpoints and how do you choose them?", "med", "Breakpoints are viewport widths where layout adjusts. Choose based on content, not device sizes. Common (content-driven): 480px (mobile), 768px (tablet), 1024px (desktop), 1280px (wide). Better approach: add breakpoints where content breaks (text wraps awkwardly, images overflow). CSS Grid with auto-fit/minmax can reduce breakpoint needs. Use consistent breakpoints across the project."],
  ["What is the difference between max-width and min-width in media queries?", "med", "min-width (mobile-first): styles apply at that width AND ABOVE (cascading upward). max-width (desktop-first): styles apply at that width AND BELOW (cascading downward). min-width queries are additive (base + override); max-width queries override base + restore on larger. min-width approach is recommended — simpler, fewer override rules, better performance."],
  ["What is the difference between ::before and ::after?", "med", "::before creates a pseudo-element as the first child of the selected element (before its content). ::after creates as the last child (after its content). Both require content property (can be empty string). Default display is inline. Commonly used for: decorative icons, clearfix, tooltips, custom checkbox styling, overlays. ::before often for left-side decorations; ::after for right-side or trailing content."],
  ["What does content property do with pseudo-elements?", "med", "The content property injects content into pseudo-elements. Values: a string (content: '★'), empty string (content: '' — required for pseudo-elements to render), attr(href) (read attribute value), open-quote/close-quote (smart quotes), url(image.svg) (image), counter() (CSS counters). content: '' is the most common — creates an empty box for decorative styling (icons, overlays, shapes)."],
  ["What is the :nth-child() selector?", "med", ":nth-child(an+b) selects elements based on their position among siblings. an+b formula: a = cycle size, b = offset. Examples: :nth-child(2) = second child, :nth-child(odd) = odd positions (1, 3, 5), :nth-child(3n+1) = every third starting at 1 (1, 4, 7...), :nth-child(-n+3) = first three. Counts ALL siblings, not just of the same type. Use :nth-of-type for same-type filtering."],
  ["What is the difference between :nth-child and :nth-of-type?", "med", ":nth-child counts ALL siblings regardless of type (if the element is the nth child overall). :nth-of-type counts only siblings of the same element type. Example: <div><p>1</p><span>2</span><p>3</p></div> — :nth-child(2) selects <span> (second child), :nth-of-type(2) selects <p>3</p> (second paragraph). :nth-of-type is usually what you want for alternating styles in mixed content."],
  ["What is the difference between > (child), + (adjacent), and ~ (sibling) combinators?", "med", "> (child combinator): selects direct children only (div > p selects p directly inside div). + (adjacent sibling): selects the immediately following sibling (h2 + p selects p right after h2). ~ (general sibling): selects ALL following siblings (h2 ~ p selects all p siblings after h2). All three are more specific than descendant selector (space) which selects any depth."],
  ["What is the overflow property and what are its values?", "med", "overflow controls what happens when content exceeds its container. visible (default) — content overflows, not clipped. hidden — content clipped, no scrollbar. scroll — always show scrollbars (clipped). auto — scrollbars shown only when needed (clipped). overflow-x and overflow-y control axes independently. overflow sets both axes. overflow: hidden also creates a block formatting context and stops margin collapsing."],
  ["What is text-overflow: ellipsis and what does it require?", "med", "text-overflow: ellipsis shows '...' when text overflows its container. Requires: white-space: nowrap (prevents wrapping) AND overflow: hidden (clips overflow). Single-line ellipsis only — doesn't work for multi-line by default. For multi-line (line-clamp): display: -webkit-box, -webkit-line-clamp: 3, -webkit-box-orient: vertical, overflow: hidden."],
  ["What is the difference between background-size: cover and contain?", "med", "cover: scales image to cover the entire container (may crop parts of image if aspect ratio differs). Image fills the container completely — no gaps. contain: scales image to fit entirely within the container (may leave empty space). No cropping — entire image visible. Use cover for full-bleed hero backgrounds; contain for product images that must show the whole subject."],
  ["How does CSS specificity handle conflicts between id, class, and element selectors?", "med", "ID selectors (#nav) add 0,1,0,0 — win against classes (0,0,1,0) and elements (0,0,0,1). A single ID beats any number of class selectors. Example: #nav (0,1,0,0) beats .menu .item .link (0,0,3,0). Avoid overusing IDs in CSS — they're too specific and hard to override. Prefer classes for reusable, maintainable styles."],
  ["What are CSS transforms? Give examples of translate, rotate, scale.", "med", "transform modifies element position and shape in 2D/3D space. transform: translateX(50px) moves right; translateY(-20px) moves up; translate(50px, -20px) both. rotate(45deg) rotates clockwise. scale(1.5) enlarges 1.5x; scaleX(2) stretches horizontally. Multiple transforms: transform: translate(50px) rotate(45deg) scale(1.2). Transforms create a new stacking context. They don't affect layout (other elements aren't affected)."],
  ["What is the difference between transform: translate() and position: relative?", "med", "Both move an element visually, but translate does NOT affect layout (element's original space is preserved — other elements don't move). position: relative moves element AND affects layout (if you move a relative element, its original space stays empty). translate is better for animations (GPU-accelerated, no reflow), while relative is simpler for static adjustments. translate creates a stacking context; relative only with z-index."],
  ["What is the BEM naming convention?", "med", "BEM (Block, Element, Modifier) is a CSS naming methodology. Block: standalone component (e.g., .btn). Element: part of a block (e.g., .btn__icon). Modifier: variant state (e.g., .btn--primary, .btn--disabled). Benefits: no specificity conflicts (all classes, same specificity), self-documenting HTML, reusable components. Prevents cascade issues common with nested selectors. Example: <button class='btn btn--primary'><span class='btn__icon'>✓</span>Submit</button>."],
  ["What is CSS Modules and what problem does it solve?", "med", "CSS Modules automatically scope class names locally by generating unique class hashes (Component.module.css → component_button_2x3y4). Solves: global CSS conflicts, naming collisions, specificity wars. Each component imports its own CSS module and uses imported styles object. Styles are scoped by default — no need for BEM. Works well with component frameworks (React, Vue). Trade-off: dynamic styles harder, no global utility classes."],
  ["What are Sass/SCSS variables vs CSS custom properties?", "med", "Sass variables ($primary) are compile-time — resolved during preprocessing, produce static values. CSS custom properties (--primary) are runtime — dynamic, can change with media queries, JavaScript, or inheritance. Sass variables are dead after compilation; CSS variables are alive in the browser. Use Sass vars for configuration; CSS vars for theming, responsive values, runtime changes."],
  ["What is the difference between Sass mixins and extends?", "med", "Mixins (@mixin + @include) COPY styles into each selector (can include parameters and logic). Extends (@extend) groups selectors together — shares the same rule block. @extend produces smaller CSS (no duplication) but can cause unintended grouping and specificity issues. Use mixins for parameterized, dynamic styles. Use extends for static shared styles where grouping is safe (like clearfix)."],
  ["What is the :is() pseudo-class?", "med", ":is() takes a selector list and applies styles to any element matching any selector in the list. :is(header, main, footer) p { ... } targets p inside any of those elements. Replaces comma-separated groups with simpler syntax. :is() takes the HIGHEST specificity of its arguments (unlike :where()). Accepts forgiving selector list (invalid selectors ignored). Useful for reducing repetition: :is(h1, h2, h3) { font-weight: bold; }."],
  ["What is the :where() pseudo-class and how does its specificity differ from :is()?", "med", ":where() works like :is() but always has ZERO specificity (0,0,0,0). :where(.important) p { color: red } — the p element selector determines specificity (0,0,0,1), not the class. This makes :where() ideal for base/default styles that should be easily overridable. :is() takes the specificity of its most specific argument. :where() zero specificity is intentional for low-priority defaults."],
  ["What is the aspect-ratio property?", "med", "aspect-ratio: width / height sets the preferred aspect ratio for an element. aspect-ratio: 16/9 maintains 16:9 ratio. Works with auto (natural size if content) or a fixed ratio. For images with native dimensions, aspect-ratio + width: 100% gives responsive images without layout shift (CLS prevention). Replaces the padding-bottom hack. Combines with object-fit for image control."],

  // ── Hard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is the difference between repaint and reflow and why does it matter?", "hard", "Reflow (layout): recalculates element positions and geometries. Triggered by: DOM changes, style changes affecting layout (width, height, position, font-size), window resize. Paint (repaint): fills pixels after layout. Triggered by: color, background, visibility, box-shadow changes. Reflow always triggers repaint but not vice versa. Reflow is expensive (recalculates subtree or entire document). Minimize reflow by: batching DOM reads/writes, using transform/opacity for animations."],
  ["Why should you only animate transform and opacity for 60fps performance?", "hard", "transform and opacity are composited properties — they don't trigger reflow or repaint. The browser promotes the element to its own compositor layer (GPU). Animating transforms (translate, scale, rotate) or opacity only needs the GPU to recomposite — ~0.5ms per frame vs 10ms+ for reflow. Animating width, height, top, left triggers layout → paint → composite (full pipeline). Rule: stay on the compositor — animate transform and opacity only."],
  ["What is GPU compositing and which CSS properties trigger it?", "hard", "GPU compositing moves rendering from CPU to GPU for faster performance. Properties that trigger compositing (promote to layer): transform (any 3D or 2D), opacity, will-change, filter, position: fixed, video/canvas elements, <iframe>. 3D transforms (translateZ, scale3d) automatically create layers. Compositing isolates the element — changes only need layer update, not page repaint. Overusing layers consumes GPU memory (bandwidth issue on mobile)."],
  ["What is will-change and when should you use it?", "hard", "will-change: transform hints the browser that the property will change soon — it creates a compositor layer in advance (avoids jank at animation start). Use sparingly — only for known upcoming animations, and remove afterwards. Overuse causes excessive layer creation (memory waste). Best practice: set will-change before animation starts, unset after: will-change: transform; then animate; then will-change: auto. Not a cure-all — only for specific performance issues."],
  ["What is layout thrashing and how do you avoid it in CSS/JS?", "hard", "Layout thrashing happens when JS repeatedly forces reflow inside a read/write loop. Example: reading element.offsetHeight, then setting height, then reading again triggers reflow each iteration. Avoid by: batching reads first (read all measurements), then writes (apply all changes). Use requestAnimationFrame to batch visual updates. Separate DOM reads from writes. Use ResizeObserver instead of scroll/resize event handlers with layout queries."],
  ["What is the critical rendering path from CSS perspective?", "hard", "Critical rendering path: bytes → DOM → CSSOM → Render Tree → Layout → Paint → Composite. CSS blocks rendering — the browser blocks page rendering until CSSOM is built (CSS is render-blocking). The browser optimizes by: sending HTML chunks, building DOM incrementally, but waiting for CSS before painting. Optimize: inline critical CSS (above-fold styles), async non-critical CSS (media='print' or media='none' + load event), preload key CSS, minify CSS."],
  ["What is render-blocking CSS and how do you optimise it?", "hard", "Render-blocking CSS prevents the browser from painting until the CSS file is downloaded and parsed. All external CSS files are render-blocking by default (<link rel='stylesheet'>). Optimize: 1) Inline critical CSS in <head>, 2) Defer non-critical CSS with media='print' (loads asynchronously, switches to all after load), 3) Preload key CSS, 4) Use media queries to block only for specific devices (print, narrow screens), 5) Split CSS by route (pages load only needed CSS)."],
  ["What is the difference between contain: layout, paint, and size?", "hard", "contain: layout — element's layout is isolated (no external layout side effects). contain: paint — element's painting is clipped to its box (no overflow paints outside). contain: size — element's size is independent of children (must have explicit size, children won't affect it). contain: strict = layout paint size. contain: content = layout paint. Use containment to limit reflow/repaint scope for performance widgets, offscreen content, and web components."],
  ["What are CSS container queries and how do they differ from media queries?", "hard", "Container queries (@container) apply styles based on a parent element's size, not the viewport. Define a containment context: container-type: inline-size on a parent. Then: @container (min-width: 400px) { .card { flex-direction: row; } }. Unlike media queries (viewport-based), container queries enable component-level responsiveness — a card reflows based on its container, regardless of page layout. Essential for reusable, responsive components."],
  ["What are CSS cascade layers (@layer) and what problem do they solve?", "hard", "Cascade layers (@layer) let authors explicitly control the order in which style origins are applied. Define layers in order: @layer base, components, utilities. Later layers override earlier ones regardless of specificity. @layer base { ... } @layer components { ... } @layer utilities { ... }. Problem solved: specificity wars between third-party CSS, reset, components, and utilities. Layers give framework authors and teams predictable override control."],
  ["What is the CSS Houdini API?", "hard", "CSS Houdini is a set of APIs that give developers low-level access to the browser's CSS engine. APIs: Painting API (PaintWorklet — custom paint functions), Layout API (LayoutWorklet — custom display modes), Properties and Values API (@property — typed CSS custom properties), Typed OM (typed CSS values instead of strings), Animation Worklet (custom animation timelines). Houdini enables polyfilling CSS features and creating custom rendering behavior not possible with standard CSS."],
  ["What is CSS Painting API (part of Houdini)?", "hard", "The CSS Painting API (CSS.paintWorklet.addModule) lets you define custom paint functions via JavaScript Canvas 2D context. Register a paint worklet: registerPaint('checkerboard', class { paint(ctx, size, properties) { ... } }). Use in CSS: background-image: paint(checkerboard). The worklet is invoked during compositing (no main thread). Useful for: complex patterns, dynamic gradients, custom backgrounds that respond to CSS variables."],
  ["What is the CSS Typed OM?", "hard", "CSS Typed Object Model (Typed OM) represents CSS values as typed JS objects instead of strings. el.attributeStyleMap.set('width', CSS.px(200)) vs el.style.width = '200px'. Methods: CSS.px(), CSS.em(), CSS.rem(), CSS.percent(), CSS.min(), CSS.clamp(). Benefits: no string parsing, type safety, mathematical operations on values, better performance (no string<->number conversion). Part of Houdini — enables metaprogramming with CSS values."],
  ["What is CSS subgrid and when would you use it?", "hard", "subgrid (grid-template-rows: subgrid / grid-template-columns: subgrid) lets a nested grid inherit track sizes from its parent grid. Instead of defining its own tracks, the child aligns to the parent's grid lines. Use when: child grid items need to align with parent grid tracks (e.g., cards in a grid where each card's internal layout aligns horizontally). Subgrid is the only way to align nested grid items with the parent grid."],
  ["What is the :has() relational pseudo-class?", "hard", ":has() selects a parent element based on its children. div:has(img) selects divs that contain an img. .card:has(.active) selects cards with an active class. Can select based on siblings: h2:has(+ p) selects h2 followed by p. :has() enables parent selection — previously impossible in CSS. Use cases: styling cards with images differently, form groups with errors, complete conditional layouts without JavaScript."],
  ["What is CSS nesting (native)?", "hard", "CSS nesting allows writing nested selectors inside a parent rule (like Sass). .card { & .title { color: blue; } &--featured { border: 2px solid; } @media (min-width: 768px) { .title { font-size: 1.5rem; } } }. The & refers to the parent selector. Nesting reduces repetition and keeps related styles together. Supported in modern browsers (2023+). Not as flexible as Sass nesting (no deep concatenation) but covers most use cases."],
  ["What is the color-mix() function in CSS?", "hard", "color-mix(in srgb, red 50%, blue) mixes two colors in a specified color space. Parameters: color space (srgb, srgb-linear, lab, oklab, xyz, etc.), color 1 with optional percentage, color 2. Example: color-mix(in oklch, var(--primary) 80%, black 20%) creates a darker primary. Useful for: generating color variants, theming, dark mode without hardcoding specific shades."],
  ["What is a logical property in CSS (e.g., margin-inline vs margin-left)?", "hard", "Logical properties map to physical directions based on writing mode. margin-inline-start = margin-left in LTR, margin-right in RTL. margin-block-start = margin-top. Properties: margin/padding/border/inset-block/inline (-start, -end). Requires writing-mode and direction awareness. Benefits: automatic RTL support without duplicating rules, better i18n. Use logical properties for internationalized layouts where text direction may vary."],
  ["What is CSS scroll-driven animations?", "hard", "Scroll-driven animations (CSS 2024) link animation progress to scroll position. animation-timeline: scroll() ties animation to scroll container. scroll() can be scroll(nearest), scroll(root). Animation progress goes from 0 (start scroll) to 1 (end scroll). Combine with @keyframes for scroll-triggered effects. view-timeline for element-intersection-based animations. Enables parallax, progress bars, reveal animations with pure CSS (no JS)."],
  ["What is the view-transition API in CSS?", "hard", "The View Transition API enables smooth animated transitions between page navigations (SPA/MPA). CSS: ::view-transition-old (old page snapshot) and ::view-transition-new (new page). Default animation: crossfade. Customize: @keyframes for individual elements, view-transition-name on specific elements for element-level transitions. Supported in Chrome 111+. MPA transitions (cross-document) available in Chrome 126+. Replaces complex JS animation orchestration."],
  ["What is the difference between transform creating a stacking context and z-index?", "hard", "transform (any non-none value) creates a NEW stacking context for the element and its children. This means the transformed element and its descendants are grouped together in a new context — their z-index values are relative to each other, not the page. z-index adjusts position within EXISTING stacking contexts. transform creates a new context; z-index operates within contexts. Both affect visual stacking but at different levels."],
  ["How does the CSS Grid auto-placement algorithm work?", "hard", "Grid auto-placement fills items into grid cells left-to-right, top-to-bottom (default). If an item has an explicit placement, later items flow around it. Algorithm: 1) place items with explicit grid positions, 2) place remaining items in order, filling each row/column. Dense mode (grid-auto-flow: dense) fills gaps left by earlier items (reorders visual layout from source order). Sparse mode (default) maintains source order — no backtracking."],
  ["What is the difference between minmax(0, 1fr) and minmax(auto, 1fr)?", "hard", "minmax(0, 1fr): FR track can shrink to 0 width (no minimum). The track doesn't respect content size — content may overflow. minmax(auto, 1fr): FR track has auto minimum (at least as wide as content). Content prevents shrinking. Default behavior: 1fr = minmax(auto, 1fr). Use minmax(0, 1fr) when you want tracks to shrink below content size (e.g., equal-width columns regardless of content). Use auto for content-aware sizing."],
  ["What is the CSS clamp() function?", "hard", "clamp(min, preferred, max) clamps a value between a minimum and maximum. font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem) — minimum 1rem, preferred 2.5vw, maximum 2rem. The value scales between min and max based on viewport width. Enables fluid typography and responsive sizing without media queries. Combines min(), max(), and clamp() for flexible responsive design."],
  ["What is CSS clip-path and how do you animate it?", "hard", "clip-path clips an element into a custom shape (non-rectangular). Values: circle(50%), ellipse(), polygon(50% 0%, 100% 100%, 0% 100%), inset(20px), url(#svg-clip). Animate clip-path by transitioning between compatible shapes (same number of points for polygon). clip-path: circle(0%) → circle(100%) reveals content. Creates a stacking context. Good for creative reveal animations, image shapes, and hover effects."],
  ["What is CSS masking vs CSS clipping?", "hard", "Masking (mask-image) uses an image/luminance to control element visibility (transparency based on mask pixel values). Clipping (clip-path) uses vector paths to define visible region (hard cut). Masking: supports gradients, semi-transparency (soft edges), multiple layers. Clipping: sharp edges only, but better performance (no per-pixel operation). Use masking for fade-outs, gradient transparency; clip-path for geometric shapes."],
  ["How does font-display: swap affect text rendering performance?", "hard", "font-display: swap tells the browser to render text with a fallback font immediately (FOUT — flash of unstyled text) and swap to the custom font when loaded. This ensures text is visible quickly (no invisible text), but causes layout shift when fonts swap. swap vs optional (swap only if cached, otherwise use fallback permanently). Trade-off: fast text visibility vs. potential layout shift. Use swap for brand fonts; optional for decorative fonts."],
  ["What are CSS custom properties limitations compared to Sass variables?", "hard", "CSS custom properties: can't be used in media queries (@media (min-width: var(--breakpoint)) doesn't work), can't be used in string manipulation or calculations without calc(), can't generate classes or loops, no conditionals. Sass variables: compile-time only (can't change at runtime), no media query responsiveness. CSS vars work in animations/transitions (dynamic); Sass vars don't. CSS vars are runtime; Sass vars are build-time."],
  ["What is the difference between isolation: isolate and creating a stacking context?", "hard", "isolation: isolate creates a new stacking context without needing other properties (transform, opacity, z-index). Unlike auto-created contexts (from transform, opacity), isolation is intentional and creates a fresh context for the element and its descendants — they don't inherit z-index from outside. isolation: isolate is the cleanest way to create a stacking context for components that need z-index isolation (dropdowns, modals, toasts)."],
  ["What is the CUBE CSS methodology?", "hard", "CUBE CSS (Composition, Utility, Block, Exception) is a CSS methodology by Andy Bell. Composition: layout (flexbox, grid, container queries). Utility: single-purpose classes (text-center, flow, wrapper) similar to Tailwind. Block: component styles (encapsulated, usually via CSS Modules). Exception: overrides (contextual tweaks). CUBE CSS emphasizes composition and utility over cascading block styles, with exceptions as the last resort. Pragmatic approach — not dogmatic."],
  ["How do you implement a dark mode toggle using CSS custom properties?", "hard", "Define theme variables on :root (light) and [data-theme='dark']. Toggle with JS: document.documentElement.dataset.theme = 'dark'. CSS: :root { --bg: white; --text: black; } [data-theme='dark'] { --bg: #1a1a2e; --text: #eee; } body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); }. For system preference: @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --bg: ... } }. Combine: JS toggle overrides system preference."],
  ["What is the prefers-color-scheme and prefers-reduced-motion media query?", "hard", "prefers-color-scheme: detects user's system theme. Values: light, dark. Use for automatic dark mode without user toggle. prefers-reduced-motion: user requests less animation. Values: no-preference, reduce. Disable unnecessary animations: @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { *, ::before, ::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms; transition-duration: 0.01ms; } }. Both are accessibility-focused — respect user's system settings."],
  ["What is PostCSS and what is it used for?", "hard", "PostCSS is a CSS post-processor with a plugin ecosystem. Transforms CSS with JS plugins. Popular plugins: Autoprefixer (add vendor prefixes), PostCSS Preset Env (future CSS syntax), PostCSS Nesting (nesting support), Tailwind CSS (built on PostCSS), CSS Modules. Not a preprocessor — works on CSS AST after it's written. Plugins can lint, optimize, transpile, and polyfill CSS. Part of most build pipelines (Vite, webpack, Next.js)."],
  ["What is the difference between CSS-in-JS and CSS Modules?", "hard", "CSS-in-JS (styled-components, Emotion): styles defined in JS files, dynamic based on props/state, runtime compilation (or zero-runtime with Linaria). CSS Modules: static .module.css files, compiled at build time, no runtime overhead. CSS-in-JS offers dynamic theming, prop-based styles, dead code elimination per component. CSS Modules offer better performance (no runtime), simpler debugging, no JS dependency for styles. Choose based on team preference and performance needs."],
  ["How do you debug CSS performance issues using Chrome DevTools?", "hard", "Performance tab: record page interactions, look for long Frames (purple = rendering, green = painting). Rendering tab: enable Paint Flashing (highlights repainted areas), Layout Shift Regions (shows CLS), Layer Borders (shows composited layers). Coverage tab: find unused CSS. Sources: open CSS file, scroll marks show used/unused code. CSS Overview panel: unused declarations, color contrast, specificity issues. Lighthouse: CSS performance audits."],

  // ── Conceptual / Industry (current) ────────────────────────────────────────
  ["When should you choose flexbox over CSS Grid and vice versa?", "med", "Flexbox for 1D layouts: navigation bars, centering, card rows, button groups, toolbars — content flows in one direction. Grid for 2D layouts: page layouts, dashboards, image galleries, form layouts — need to control both rows and columns simultaneously. Flexbox is content-driven (items follow content); Grid is layout-driven (items placed in grid cells). Use both together: Grid for page layout, Flexbox for components within grid cells."],
  ["Can flexbox and grid be combined in the same layout? How?", "med", "Yes. Use Grid for the outer page layout (header, sidebar, main, footer) and Flexbox inside grid items for component alignment. Example: page-grid { display: grid; grid-template: 'header header' 'sidebar main' 'footer footer'; }. Inside main, use flexbox for card rows. Both are CSS display values that can be nested freely."],
  ["What problems do container queries solve that media queries cannot?", "med", "Media queries only respond to viewport size. Container queries respond to parent container size, enabling component-level responsiveness. A card component in a narrow sidebar vs a wide main content area needs different layouts — media queries can't handle this; container queries can. Component libraries benefit greatly — reusable components adapt to wherever they're placed."],
  ["How does container-type: inline-size differ from size?", "hard", "container-type: inline-size creates a containment context only on the inline axis (width in LTR, height in vertical writing mode). container-type: size creates a context on BOTH axes (requires intrinsic size to be blocked). size is more restrictive and may break layouts if the container doesn't have explicit sizing. inline-size is the recommended default — most container queries only need width."],
  ["What are practical use cases for the :has() selector in real UIs?", "med", "Card hover state based on which child is hovered: .card:has(img:hover) { background: ... }. Form group error styling: .form-group:has(:invalid) { border-color: red }. List item with active child: li:has(.active) { font-weight: bold }. Table row with checkbox checked: tr:has(input:checked) { background: highlight }. Parent selection based on any child state — previously required JS."],
  ["How does subgrid let nested grids align to the parent grid?", "hard", "Subgrid makes the child grid's tracks match the parent grid's tracks. Instead of defining its own column/row sizes, the child uses subgrid: grid-template-columns: subgrid. The child's grid items align exactly to parent grid lines. Example: a page grid with 3 columns; each section inside also has 3 subgrid columns — all text aligns vertically across sections regardless of section content."],
  ["Why are logical properties important for internationalization (RTL/LTR)?", "med", "Logical properties (margin-inline-start, padding-block-end) automatically adapt to writing direction. Without them, RTL support requires duplicating rules: .card { margin-left: 1rem; } [dir='rtl'] .card { margin-left: 0; margin-right: 1rem; }. With logical: .card { margin-inline-end: 1rem; } — works in both LTR and RTL. Essential for i18n-ready codebases."],
  ["How do cascade layers change specificity resolution between layers?", "hard", "Layers (@layer base, components, utilities) are resolved BEFORE specificity. All rules in later layers win over all rules in earlier layers regardless of specificity. A #id in 'base' loses to a .class in 'components' (because layer order comes first). Within the same layer, normal specificity rules apply. Layers also support nested layers (@layer components.buttons). Layer order is determined by declaration order or @layer order statement."],
  ["What is the recommended ordering strategy for @layer declarations?", "med", "Recommendation: @layer reset, base, tokens, components, utilities, overrides. Or simpler: @layer base, components, utilities. The order depends on your methodology. Typically: reset/normalize first, then base styles (typography), then design tokens, then component styles, then utility framework (Tailwind), then overrides. Use @layer [...] list at the top of your stylesheet to define the order before any rules."],
  ["How do you reduce CSS bundle size in a large production app?", "hard", "1) Purge unused CSS (Tailwind JIT, PurgeCSS), 2) Code-split CSS per route (CSS Modules, lazy-loading), 3) Minify (cssnano), 4) Remove redundant prefixes (Autoprefixer with target browsers), 5) Use CSS custom properties instead of repeated values, 6) Avoid large third-party CSS frameworks (use only what you need), 7) Compress (Gzip, Brotli), 8) Use modern CSS features to replace hacks (aspect-ratio replaces padding-bottom)."],
  ["What is critical CSS and how do you inline it for above-the-fold content?", "hard", "Critical CSS extracts styles needed for above-the-fold (initial viewport) content and inlines them in <head>. Below-fold CSS loads asynchronously. Tools: Critical (Node.js), Penthouse, purgeCSS-based. Benefits: eliminates render-blocking CSS, improves FCP/LCP. Implementation: build step extracts critical CSS, inlines in HTML, loads main CSS with media='print' then switches to all. Trade-off: build complexity, must re-extract for each route."],
  ["How does CSS containment improve rendering performance at scale?", "hard", "contain: layout tells the browser the element's layout won't affect outside elements — reflows inside the contained element don't propagate. contain: paint clips painting to the element's bounds. contain: size lets the browser skip layout of offscreen children. Used together (contain: strict), the browser can skip expensive layout/paint work. content-visibility: auto combines containment with lazy rendering — skips rendering of offscreen sections until near the viewport."],
  ["What is content-visibility: auto and how does it boost performance?", "hard", "content-visibility: auto applies layout, paint, and size containment to elements. The browser skips rendering of elements far outside the viewport (no layout, paint, or compositing for offscreen content). When the element scrolls near the viewport, rendering happens lazily. Boosts initial load performance significantly for long pages (2-5x improvement). Must set contain-intrinsic-size to reserve space and prevent scrollbar jank."],
  ["What strategies make a layout responsive without media queries?", "med", "Flexbox wrap: flex-wrap: wrap + flex: min-width. CSS Grid: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)). Clamp() for fluid sizing: font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem). Relative units (%, vw, vh, rem, em). Aspect-ratio for proportional sizing. Min/max width on containers. These techniques create fluid, content-driven layouts that adapt to any viewport without explicit breakpoints."],
  ["How do clamp(), min(), and max() enable fluid typography?", "med", "clamp(min, preferred, max) sets a fluid range: font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.5rem + 2vw, 2rem). The preferred value (often calc with vw) scales between min and max. min() takes the smallest: width: min(100%, 1200px) — container up to 1200px, else fills width. max() takes largest. Combination: width: min(max(300px, 50%), 800px) creates a responsive range. These enable fluid, container-aware sizing without media queries."],
  ["What accessibility issues can arise from CSS-only hiding techniques?", "med", "display: none — hidden from all (screen reader, focus). visibility: hidden — hidden visually, still in accessibility tree? No — it's also hidden from screen readers. opacity: 0 — visible to screen readers but transparent — can still receive focus (confusing). clip/position offscreen — visible to screen readers (used for screen-reader-only text, correct use). Use clip: rect(0,0,0,0) for 'visually hidden but accessible' (screen-reader-only labels). Never use visibility:hidden on focusable elements."],
  ["How do you build an accessible focus-visible outline strategy?", "med", "Use :focus-visible for keyboard-only focus indicators. CSS: :focus { outline: none; } :focus-visible { outline: 2px solid blue; outline-offset: 2px; }. :focus-visible only shows the outline when the user navigates with keyboard (Tab key), not on mouse click. Provide: strong contrast (3:1), sufficient width (2-3px), offset from element. Avoid: removing outline entirely (breaks keyboard navigation), low-contrast outlines, outline: auto (browser-dependent visibility)."],
  ["What is the difference between :focus and :focus-visible?", "med", ":focus applies whenever an element receives focus (mouse click, keyboard tab, programmatic). :focus-visible only applies when the browser determines focus should be visible (keyboard navigation). :focus-visible doesn't show for mouse clicks — prevents the ugly blue ring after every click. Use :focus-visible for focus indicators; keep :focus for fallback (older browsers). This improves UX for mouse users while maintaining keyboard accessibility."],
  ["Why should you respect prefers-reduced-motion in animations?", "med", "Users with vestibular disorders can experience dizziness, nausea, and pain from animations. prefers-reduced-motion: reduce signals the user wants minimal movement. CSS: @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; scroll-behavior: auto !important; } }. Essential for accessibility compliance (WCAG 2.3.3). Consider offering a toggle for reduced motion in complex apps."],
  ["How do you ensure sufficient color contrast for WCAG compliance via CSS?", "med", "WCAG AA requires: 4.5:1 contrast ratio for normal text, 3:1 for large text (18px+ bold or 24px+ regular). WCAG AAA: 7:1 normal, 4.5:1 large. CSS: use contrast-checking tools (WebAIM, Chrome DevTools), use near-black on near-white (not pure #000 on #fff which is 21:1 — causes eye strain). Use CSS custom properties for color palette and check all combinations. For components: ensure hover/focus states also meet contrast minimums."],
  ["What is the accent-color property and what does it style?", "easy", "accent-color sets the color of form controls (checkbox, radio, range, progress) with a single property. accent-color: #007bff turns native checkboxes blue. Works with forced-colors for accessibility. Simplifies form styling — no need for custom checkbox pseudo-elements for basic color changes. Supported in modern browsers (Chrome 93+, Firefox 92+, Safari 15.4+)."],
  ["What are the new wide-gamut color spaces (oklch, lab, lch) in CSS?", "hard", "Wide-gamut color spaces display colors beyond sRGB — visible on P3 displays (modern phones, laptops). oklch: perceptual lightness (L), chroma (C, saturation), hue (H). Preferred for accessible palettes (lightness matches human perception). lab/lch: CIE Lab/Lch, wider gamut than sRGB, device-independent. Use: color: oklch(70% 0.15 250) — lighter blue with defined saturation. These spaces give access to 50%+ more colors than sRGB."],
  ["Why is oklch preferred for creating accessible color palettes?", "hard", "oklch separates lightness (L) from chroma (C) and hue (H). Lightness is perceptually uniform — equal L values appear equally bright to human eye. This makes it easy to create: accessible contrast (compute L difference), dark mode (invert L only), consistent saturation across hues (same C gives same vibrancy). oklch avoids the blue-too-dark problem of HSL (blue at same L as red appears darker). Essential for modern design systems."],
  ["What does the gap property do in flexbox and grid?", "easy", "gap (previously grid-gap) defines spacing between rows and columns. gap: 1rem = row-gap + column-gap both 1rem. gap: 1rem 2rem = row-gap 1rem, column-gap 2rem. Works in flex (now supported in modern browsers) and grid. Unlike margins, gap doesn't add space at edges (no extra space before first or after last item). gap is the preferred way to space flex/grid items."],
  ["What is the difference between place-items and place-content?", "med", "place-items is shorthand for align-items + justify-items (aligns items within their grid cells). place-content is shorthand for align-content + justify-content (distributes space between grid tracks). place-items controls item positioning within cells; place-content controls track distribution. In flexbox, place-items works (aligns items), place-content also works (distributes lines). Both are grid/flex properties but affect different aspects."],
  ["What are CSS scroll snap properties and when are they useful?", "med", "scroll-snap-type (container) and scroll-snap-align (children) create snap points where scrolling stops. Container: scroll-snap-type: x mandatory (horizontal, must snap), or proximity (snap if close). Children: scroll-snap-align: start (snap to start edge). Useful for: image carousels, horizontal card scroll, section-by-section vertical pages, product galleries. Improves UX — controlled scrolling without JS libraries."],
  ["How do sticky table headers work with position: sticky?", "med", "position: sticky on <th> makes table headers stick during vertical scroll. Requires: <thead> or <th> with position: sticky; top: 0. The <thead> must be sticky container. Works in modern browsers. Issue: CSS 2.1 table rendering doesn't support sticky on thead in some older implementations — use separate sticky div or use display: flex table alternatives for old browsers."],
  ["What is the inset shorthand property?", "easy", "inset is shorthand for top, right, bottom, left (like margin/padding shorthand). inset: 0 = all sides 0. inset: 10px 20px = top/bottom 10px, left/right 20px. inset: 10px 20px 30px 40px = top, right, bottom, left. Only works on positioned elements (position: relative/absolute/fixed/sticky). Useful for: full-cover absolute overlays, sticky positioning offsets, alignment."],
  ["How do CSS counters work for numbered lists or sections?", "med", "CSS counters auto-number elements via CSS. counter-reset: counter-name (create/reset counter). counter-increment: counter-name (increment). content: counter(counter-name) display value. Example: body { counter-reset: section; } h2::before { counter-increment: section; content: 'Section ' counter(section) ': '; }. Nested counters: counter(name, style) with decimal, lower-roman, etc. Good for complex auto-numbering without HTML changes."],
  ["What is the @supports feature query and how do you use it for fallbacks?", "med", "@supports (property: value) { ... } tests browser support before applying styles. @supports (display: grid) { .container { display: grid; } } @supports not (display: grid) { .container { display: flex; } }. Also: @supports selector(:has(*)). Used for progressive enhancement — provide advanced styles for supporting browsers, fallbacks for older ones. Reduces need for JS feature detection for CSS features."],

  // ── Code / Implementation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["Write CSS to perfectly center a div both horizontally and vertically.", "easy", "Flexbox: .parent { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; height: 100vh; }. Grid: .parent { display: grid; place-items: center; height: 100vh; }. Absolute: .parent { position: relative; } .child { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }. Flexbox/Grid are preferred — no position dependency."],
  ["How would you center a div using only margin auto?", "easy", "Horizontal centering: .child { width: fit-content; margin: 0 auto; }. Only works horizontally on block elements with explicit width. Vertical centering with margin: auto only works with flexbox (align-items: center) or absolute positioning (top: 0; bottom: 0; margin: auto). Pure margin: auto for both axes requires a positioned parent and specific constraints."],
  ["Write CSS to create a responsive 3-column grid that collapses to 1 on mobile.", "med", ".grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1rem; } @media (max-width: 768px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }. Or fluid without media queries: .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }. The minmax approach auto-collapses based on available width — no breakpoints needed."],
  ["Build a responsive grid with grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)).", "med", ".gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; } .gallery img { width: 100%; height: 200px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 8px; }. auto-fit fills the row with as many 200px columns as possible, expanding equally if space remains. On small screens, collapses to 1 column. No media queries needed. Add container query support for component-level responsiveness."],
  ["Write CSS for a sticky footer that stays at the bottom on short pages.", "med", "Using flexbox: body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 100vh; } main { flex: 1; } footer { padding: 1rem; }. Using Grid: body { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto; min-height: 100vh; }. The flex: 1 (or 1fr) pushes the footer down when content is short, and expands naturally when content is long."],
  ["Write CSS to truncate text to a single line with an ellipsis.", "easy", ".truncate { white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }. Requires: a defined width (or block with max-width). Without width, the text won't know when to truncate. Use max-width: 100% on flexible containers. For responsive truncation: width: 100% or container-based sizing."],
  ["Write CSS to clamp text to 3 lines with an ellipsis (line-clamp).", "med", ".line-clamp-3 { display: -webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp: 3; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; overflow: hidden; }. The -webkit-line-clamp property (standardized but still uses -webkit prefix) limits display to N lines and appends ellipsis. Requires display: -webkit-box and overflow: hidden. Fallback for unsupported browsers: set max-height based on line-height."],
  ["How would you create a square element that scales with viewport width?", "med", "Using aspect-ratio: .square { aspect-ratio: 1; width: 50%; }. Using padding-bottom hack (older method): .square { width: 50%; padding-bottom: 50%; height: 0; }. The padding-bottom trick works because padding percentage is relative to parent width. aspect-ratio is the cleaner modern approach."],
  ["Write CSS to maintain a 16:9 aspect ratio for a video container.", "med", ".video-wrapper { position: relative; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16/9; } .video-wrapper iframe { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }. Modern: .video-wrapper { aspect-ratio: 16/9; } iframe { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }. aspect-ratio replaces the padding-bottom: 56.25% hack."],
  ["Write CSS to create a custom checkbox using accent-color or pseudo-elements.", "med", "Simple (accent-color): input[type='checkbox'] { accent-color: #007bff; width: 20px; height: 20px; }. Custom (pseudo-elements): input[type='checkbox'] { appearance: none; width: 20px; height: 20px; border: 2px solid #ccc; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; } input[type='checkbox']:checked { background: #007bff; border-color: #007bff; } input[type='checkbox']:checked::after { content: '✓'; color: white; display: block; text-align: center; }."],
  ["How would you build a pure-CSS tooltip on hover?", "med", ".tooltip { position: relative; cursor: pointer; } .tooltip::after { content: attr(data-tooltip); position: absolute; bottom: 100%; left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%); background: #333; color: white; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14px; white-space: nowrap; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; transition: opacity 0.2s; } .tooltip:hover::after { opacity: 1; }. Use ::before for arrow."],
  ["Write CSS to create a fade-in animation on page load.", "easy", "@keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(10px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } } .fade-in { animation: fadeIn 0.5s ease forwards; } .fade-in-delayed { animation: fadeIn 0.5s ease 0.2s forwards; opacity: 0; }. For staggered children: .item:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.1s; } .item:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.2s; } etc."],
  ["Write a @keyframes animation that bounces an element vertically.", "med", "@keyframes bounce { 0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); } 50% { transform: translateY(-20px); } } .bouncer { animation: bounce 1s ease-in-out infinite; }. For a more natural bounce: @keyframes bounce { 0% { transform: translateY(0); } 30% { transform: translateY(-30px); } 50% { transform: translateY(-15px); } 70% { transform: translateY(-25px); } 100% { transform: translateY(0); } }. Use cubic-bezier for more realistic physics."],
  ["How would you create a CSS-only accordion using <details>?", "med", "<details class='accordion'> <summary>Section 1</summary> <div class='content'>Content here...</div> </details>. CSS: details { border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 4px; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; } summary { padding: 1rem; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; } .content { padding: 0 1rem 1rem; } details[open] summary { border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; }."],
  ["Write CSS to make an image cover its container without distortion.", "easy", ".cover-image { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }. For a container with fixed aspect ratio: .container { width: 100%; height: 300px; overflow: hidden; } .container img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }. object-fit: cover crops excess; object-fit: contain shows full image with letterboxing. Add object-position for focal point control."],
  ["How would you implement dark mode using prefers-color-scheme?", "med", ":root { --bg: #ffffff; --text: #1a1a1a; --primary: #0066cc; } @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --bg: #1a1a2e; --text: #e0e0e0; --primary: #66b3ff; } } body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); } .btn { background: var(--primary); }. Add user toggle: [data-theme='dark'] overrides media query with higher specificity, or use JS to set data-theme and remove prefers-color-scheme reliance."],
  ["Write CSS variables for a theme and switch them with a data-theme attribute.", "med", ":root, [data-theme='light'] { --bg: #fff; --text: #222; --surface: #f5f5f5; } [data-theme='dark'] { --bg: #121212; --text: #e0e0e0; --surface: #1e1e1e; } body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); } .card { background: var(--surface); }. JS toggle: document.documentElement.dataset.theme = 'dark'. Persist in localStorage."],
  ["How would you build a responsive navbar that becomes a hamburger menu (CSS part)?", "med", ".navbar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: 1rem; } .nav-links { display: flex; gap: 1rem; list-style: none; } .hamburger { display: none; } @media (max-width: 768px) { .nav-links { display: none; flex-direction: column; } .nav-links.active { display: flex; } .hamburger { display: block; } }. Use a checkbox hack or JS to toggle .active class."],
  ["Write CSS to create a two-column layout where the sidebar is fixed width.", "med", "Grid: .layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr; min-height: 100vh; } .sidebar { background: #f0f0f0; } .main { padding: 2rem; }. Flexbox: .layout { display: flex; } .sidebar { width: 250px; flex-shrink: 0; } .main { flex: 1; }. Grid is cleaner for this two-dimensional page layout. For responsive: @media (max-width: 768px) { .layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }."],
  ["Write a grid layout using named grid-template-areas for header/sidebar/main/footer.", "med", ".layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto; grid-template-areas: 'header header' 'sidebar main' 'footer footer'; min-height: 100vh; gap: 0; } .header { grid-area: header; } .sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; } .main { grid-area: main; } .footer { grid-area: footer; } @media (max-width: 768px) { .layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; grid-template-areas: 'header' 'main' 'sidebar' 'footer'; } }."],
  ["How would you create equal-height cards in a flex row?", "med", ".card-row { display: flex; gap: 1rem; } .card { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; background: white; border: 1px solid #eee; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem; } .card-content { flex: 1; /* pushes card actions to bottom */ }. flex: 1 ensures all cards are same width. display: flex on card with flex-direction: column allows flex: 1 on content to push action buttons to bottom — making cards equal height even with different content lengths."],
  ["Write CSS to overlay a semi-transparent gradient on top of an image.", "med", ".hero { position: relative; width: 100%; height: 500px; overflow: hidden; } .hero img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; } .hero::after { content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(0,0,0,0.7) 0%, transparent 50%); pointer-events: none; /* allows clicking through overlay */ }. Or use multiple backgrounds: .hero { background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(0,0,0,0.7), transparent), url('image.jpg') center/cover; }."],
  ["How would you create a loading spinner with pure CSS?", "med", ".spinner { width: 40px; height: 40px; border: 4px solid #f3f3f3; border-top-color: #3498db; border-radius: 50%; animation: spin 0.8s linear infinite; } @keyframes spin { to { transform: rotate(360deg); } }. Variants: multiple border colors, different sizes. For a dot pulse: @keyframes pulse { 0%, 100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 1; } 50% { transform: scale(0.8); opacity: 0.5; } }."],
  ["How would you use CSS to style the first letter of a paragraph as a drop cap?", "med", "p::first-letter { font-size: 3em; font-weight: bold; float: left; margin-right: 8px; line-height: 0.8; color: #333; }. ::first-letter targets only the first typographic letter. Combine with ::first-line for complementary styles. For browsers that support initial-letter: p::first-letter { initial-letter: 2; } — simpler but less widely supported."],
  ["How would you horizontally scroll a row of cards with scroll-snap?", "med", ".scroll-row { display: flex; overflow-x: auto; scroll-snap-type: x mandatory; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; scrollbar-width: thin; } .scroll-card { flex: 0 0 300px; /* fixed card width */ scroll-snap-align: start; border-radius: 8px; }. Remove scrollbar: .scroll-row::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }. scroll-snap makes cards snap into place, like a native app carousel."],
  ["Write CSS to create a button with a hover scale and smooth transition.", "easy", ".btn { padding: 12px 24px; background: #007bff; color: white; border: none; border-radius: 6px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 1rem; transition: all 0.2s ease; } .btn:hover { transform: scale(1.05); box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,123,255,0.3); } .btn:active { transform: scale(0.98); }. Use transform (composited) for performance. Add focus-visible: .btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #0056b3; outline-offset: 2px; }."],
  ["How would you make a fluid font size between 1rem and 2rem using clamp()?", "med", "font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.5rem + 2vw, 2rem). At 375px viewport: ~1.25rem. At 768px: ~1.58rem. At 1200px+: 2rem. The formula: min + (max-min) * (100vw - minViewportWidth) / (maxViewportWidth - minViewportWidth). Calc approach: font-size: calc(1rem + 1vw) — simpler but no min/max clamping. clamp() is preferred — prevents text from getting too small or too large."],
  ["Write CSS to give an element a focus ring only for keyboard users.", "med", "/* Base: remove default */ :focus { outline: none; } /* Keyboard: show high-contrast ring */ :focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #005fcc; outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: 4px; } /* Fallback for older browsers */ :focus { outline: 2px solid #005fcc; outline-offset: 2px; } @supports not selector(:focus-visible) { :focus { outline: 2px solid #005fcc; } } @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { :focus-visible { transition: outline-offset 0.1s ease; } }."],
  ["How would you build a responsive image gallery masonry layout in CSS Grid?", "hard", ".masonry { columns: 4; column-gap: 1rem; } .masonry-item { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: 1rem; } .masonry-item img { width: 100%; display: block; border-radius: 8px; } @media (max-width: 1024px) { .masonry { columns: 3; } } @media (max-width: 768px) { .masonry { columns: 2; } } @media (max-width: 480px) { .masonry { columns: 1; } }. CSS columns create a true masonry (vertical fill) unlike Grid which forces rows. Grid masonry (subgrid) is still experimental."],
  ["Write CSS to create a triangle shape using borders.", "med", ".triangle { width: 0; height: 0; border-left: 25px solid transparent; border-right: 25px solid transparent; border-bottom: 50px solid #333; }. No width/height, borders create a triangle. Direction: use border-left/border-right for up/down triangles. Different sizes create different angles. Right triangle: .triangle-right { border-top: 25px solid transparent; border-bottom: 25px solid transparent; border-left: 50px solid #333; }."],
  ["How would you pin an element to the bottom of the viewport on scroll?", "med", "Sticky: .element { position: sticky; bottom: 0; z-index: 10; }. Works within the scrolling parent. For a truly viewport-fixed element regardless of scroll position: .element { position: fixed; bottom: 0; left: 0; right: 0; z-index: 10; }. Fixed is viewport-relative regardless of content. Sticky is container-relative and stops at parent boundary."],
  ["Write CSS to disable text selection on an element.", "easy", ".no-select { -webkit-user-select: none; -moz-user-select: none; -ms-user-select: none; user-select: none; }. Use for: UI elements (buttons, icons, drag handles), interfaces that feel more app-like. Don't use on: main text content (hurts accessibility — users can't copy text). user-select: text re-enables on child elements."],
  ["How would you create a striped table with :nth-child(even)?", "easy", "table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; } th, td { padding: 12px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; } tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: #f9f9f9; } tbody tr:hover { background: #f1f1f1; }. nth-child(odd) for odd rows. Combine with hover for interactive tables. For more stripes: use nth-child(4n+2) (2nd and 6th in 4-pattern)."],
  ["Write CSS to make a div take the full remaining height in a flex column.", "med", ".flex-column { display: flex; flex-direction: column; height: 100vh; } .header { height: 60px; flex-shrink: 0; } .content { flex: 1; overflow-y: auto; } .footer { height: 40px; flex-shrink: 0; }. flex: 1 on content makes it fill remaining space after header and footer. overflow-y: auto for scrollable content. For grid: .layout { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto; height: 100vh; }."],
  ["How would you implement a print stylesheet that hides navigation?", "med", "@media print { @page { margin: 2cm; } body { font-size: 12pt; } nav, .sidebar, .footer, .ads, .no-print { display: none !important; } a[href]::after { content: ' (' attr(href) ')'; font-size: 10pt; } a[href^='#']::after { content: ''; } /* skip anchor links */ img { max-width: 100%; page-break-inside: avoid; } h1, h2, h3 { page-break-after: avoid; } }."],
  // ── Tricky output / behavior ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  [`What wins: #id vs .class.class.class on the same element?`, "med", "#id wins. Specificity: #id = (0,1,0,0), .class.class.class = (0,0,3,0). A single ID beats any number of class selectors (IDs win at the second level). To override an ID selector in a maintainable way: avoid using IDs in CSS in the first place."],
  [`Specificity: a, b { color: red } vs a.link { color: blue } on a.link — which color?`, "med", "blue wins. a, b { color: red } becomes two separate rules each with specificity (0,0,0,1) — element selector only. a.link { color: blue } has specificity (0,0,1,1) — one class + one element. The class makes it more specific."],
  [`Does :where(.a) add any specificity? What color wins vs a plain element selector?`, "hard", ":where(.a) adds ZERO specificity (0,0,0,0). :where(.a) p { color: red } has specificity (0,0,0,1) (only the element selector p). A plain p { color: blue } also has (0,0,0,1). Since they tie, the later rule wins. So if :where(.a) p comes first and plain p comes later, blue wins. This makes :where() ideal for low-priority defaults."],
  [`If margin-top: 20px and the child margin-top: 30px collapse, what is the gap?`, "med", "30px — the larger margin wins. Margins collapse to the maximum value between adjacent elements (or parent/child). 20px and 30px collapse to 30px. They don't add (20+30=50). Negative margins collapse differently: 20px + -10px = 10px (adds positive and negative). If both negative: -20px + -10px = -20px (most negative wins, -20)."],
  [`p { color: red !important } and inline style color: blue — which wins?`, "med", "red wins. !important overrides inline styles. The cascade order: !important author > inline style > normal author. So !important beats inline. To override !important: use another !important with higher specificity or later in source. Avoid !important whenever possible — it breaks natural cascade."],
  [`div { width: 100px; padding: 20px; box-sizing: border-box } — what is content width?`, "med", "Content width = 60px. box-sizing: border-box means width includes content + padding + border. width: 100px = content + 20px padding-left + 20px padding-right = 60px content. Without border-box, content would be 100px, total width 140px. border-box makes sizing intuitive — the element is always 100px wide."],
  [`Two adjacent inline-block divs have a gap. Why, and how do you remove it?`, "med", "The gap is whitespace in HTML between the elements (newlines/spaces are rendered as a single space, ~4px). Fixes: 1) Remove whitespace in HTML (<div>...</div><div>...</div>), 2) font-size: 0 on parent (set font-size on children), 3) use flexbox instead (gap doesn't have this issue), 4) negative margins, 5) HTML comments to separate elements."],
  [`flex item with flex: 0 1 200px in a 100px container — what width renders?`, "hard", "100px. flex: 0 1 200px = flex-grow: 0 (won't grow), flex-shrink: 1 (can shrink), flex-basis: 200px. The item's basis is 200px, but the container is only 100px. Since flex-shrink: 1, the item shrinks to fit the container. With only one item, it shrinks to 100px (container width). With flex-shrink: 0, it would overflow at 200px."],
  [`Element with position: absolute and no positioned ancestor — what is it relative to?`, "med", "The initial containing block — typically the viewport (not the document root). In most browsers, this is the <html> element's containing block, which has the same dimensions as the viewport, but absolute positioning is relative to the initial containing block, not the viewport. Fixed positioning is always relative to the viewport."],
  [`z-index: 9999 on a static-positioned element — does it apply? Why or why not?`, "med", "No, z-index does not work on elements with position: static (default). z-index only applies to positioned elements (relative, absolute, fixed, sticky) and flex/grid items. The 9999 value is ignored. To make z-index work: add position: relative (without offsets) — this makes the element positioned without changing its visual position."],
  [`opacity: 0.5 on a parent — can a child have opacity 1? What renders?`, "hard", "The child cannot exceed the parent's opacity. opacity: 0.5 on parent makes the parent and all children 50% transparent as a group — the child with opacity: 1 effectively has 0.5 (50% of parent's 50% = 25% transparent? No — child's final opacity is 1 * 0.5 = 0.5). opacity creates a stacking context and flattens the entire subtree as a single layer. To have child at full opacity: use rgba background on parent instead (doesn't affect children)."],
  [`transform: translateZ(0) on an element — what side effect does it create?`, "hard", "It creates a new stacking context and promotes the element to its own GPU compositor layer. translateZ(0) is a 3D transform, which triggers GPU acceleration. The element is painted on its own layer, separate from the page. Benefits: smoother animations (GPU-composited, no repaint), but costs: increased GPU memory usage. This is the legacy 'hardware acceleration hack' — now use will-change instead."],
  [`height: 100% on a child whose parent has no explicit height — what happens?`, "med", "height: 100% doesn't work — the child height becomes 0 (or auto, based on content). Percentage heights require the parent to have an explicit height. If parent has no height, the browser can't resolve the percentage. Fix: set parent height (100vh, 100%, or fixed), or use flexbox (align-items: stretch gives full height). For viewport height: min-height: 100vh on parent."],
  [`width: auto vs width: 100% on a block element with padding — any difference?`, "med", "Yes. width: auto respects the parent's content area and subtracts margin/padding/border automatically (no overflow). width: 100% sets the content area to exactly 100% of parent's content width, so padding/border ADD to that, causing overflow. Example: parent 500px, child padding 20px — width: auto gives content 460px (500-40), width: 100% gives content 500px + 40px padding = 540px (overflow). Prefer width: auto for block elements."],
  [`Last-listed CSS rule with equal specificity — which wins, first or last?`, "easy", "The LAST rule wins. When specificity is equal, the cascade uses source order — the rule that appears later in the stylesheet (or later <link>) wins. This is the 'Cascade' in Cascading Style Sheets. Order of <link> tags matters, order of <style> blocks matters, and the order of rules within a file matters."],
  [`color: inherit vs color: initial on a child — what is the difference in result?`, "med", "inherit forces the child to use the parent's computed value (whatever it is). initial resets to the property's default value (for color, that's black in most browsers, not the inherited value). Example: blue parent → color: inherit gives blue; color: initial gives black (or browser default, not blue). inherit takes parent's value; initial takes CSS specification default."],
  [`em font-size on nested elements — why does the text keep growing?`, "med", "em is relative to the PARENT's font-size. Each nested element multiplies: root 16px → div with font-size: 1.5em = 24px → child div with font-size: 1.5em = 36px (24 * 1.5) → grandchild = 54px. The effect compounds. Use rem (root em) for consistent sizing regardless of nesting, or reset font-size on nested elements to break the chain."],
  [`vw units and a horizontal scrollbar — why does 100vw sometimes overflow?`, "hard", "100vw includes the vertical scrollbar width (~17px on desktop). This causes horizontal overflow — the element is wider than the <html> width. Fix: use width: 100% instead of 100vw, or use CSS: html { overflow-x: hidden; }. 100vw = viewport width including scrollbar; 100% = parent content area width. For full-width elements: use width: 100% on full-width containers, not 100vw."],
  [`grid with grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr and a 300px item — does it overflow?`, "hard", "Yes, if the container is less than 600px + gaps. The fr unit distributes remaining space AFTER auto-sized content. If an item has intrinsic size larger than its 1fr share, it can't shrink below its min-content size. 1fr = minmax(auto, 1fr) — the min is auto (content size). To force equal columns regardless of content: use minmax(0, 1fr)."],
  [`flex-wrap: nowrap with items wider than container — what happens to them?`, "med", "Items shrink to fit (flex-shrink: 1 by default). If items have content that prevents shrinking (long text, images), they overflow the container. To fix: add min-width: 0 on flex items to allow shrinking below content size, or use overflow: hidden. With flex-wrap: wrap, items wrap to next line instead of shrinking/overflowing."],
  [`justify-content: space-between with one flex item — where does it sit?`, "med", "At the start (not centered). space-between distributes space BETWEEN items — with one item, there's no other item to distribute between, so it sits at flex-start. For one item to center: use justify-content: center, or add an invisible spacer element. space-around/space-evenly also center a single item (they account for space around each item)."],
  [`A float: left element with no clearfix — why does the parent collapse?`, "med", "Floated elements are removed from normal flow — the parent doesn't see them and collapses to 0 height (if no non-floated content). Fix (clearfix): .parent::after { content: ''; display: table; clear: both; }. Or: overflow: hidden on parent (creates block formatting context). Modern alternative: use flexbox or grid instead of float layouts."],
  [`overflow: hidden on a parent — how does it affect a position: sticky child?`, "hard", "overflow: hidden on a parent creates a new scroll container, which clips position: sticky — the sticky child stops working because sticky is relative to the nearest scroll container. Similarly, overflow: auto, overflow: scroll, or overflow: clip on any ancestor breaks position: sticky. Fix: don't use overflow: hidden on sticky-ancestor elements, or use a different layout approach."],
  [`white-space: nowrap on a flex item — does it shrink below content? What renders?`, "hard", "No, the flex item cannot shrink below its content size (min-width: auto default). white-space: nowrap prevents text from wrapping, so inline content stays on one line. The item's min-width is set by the content width. The item will overflow or cause the flex container to expand. Fix: min-width: 0 on the flex item, or overflow: hidden for truncation."],
  [`min-width: auto default on flex items — why can content overflow the container?`, "hard", "Flex items have min-width: auto (or min-height: auto for column) by default — which sets the minimum size to the content's intrinsic size. Even if flex-shrink is applied, items can't shrink below their content. This causes overflow when content is longer than available space. Fix: min-width: 0 (or min-height: 0) on the flex item to allow it to shrink below content size."],
  [`::before with no content property — does it render? Why or why not?`, "med", "No, ::before and ::after require the content property to render (even if empty string). Without content: '', the pseudo-element has no box — display: block or width/height won't make it appear. Always set content: '' for decorative pseudo-elements. For text content, set content: 'text'. The only exception: content: none explicitly prevents rendering."],
  [`a:hover and a:visited order in CSS — why might hover not work? (LVHA rule)`, "med", "LVHA order: :link → :visited → :hover → :active. If :hover comes before :visited, :visited has the same specificity and comes later, so visited links show only the :visited style (hover effect hidden). Fix: always use LVHA order: a:link, a:visited, a:hover, a:active. Or use :any-link instead of :link/:visited for simpler rules."],
  [`line-height: 1.5 vs line-height: 150% inheritance — what differs in children?`, "hard", "line-height: 1.5 (unitless) — children inherit the factor, computed relative to each child's font-size (child 20px → 30px, child 30px → 45px). line-height: 150% (percentage) — computed for the parent FIRST (parent 16px → 24px), then that computed VALUE is inherited by children (all children get 24px regardless of their font-size). Unitless is the recommended approach for inheritance."],
  [`background and background-color shorthand conflict — which value applies?`, "med", "background shorthand OVERRIDES background-color if set after. background: url(image.jpg) sets ALL background sub-properties (including background-color to transparent). So .el { background-color: red; background: url(image.jpg); } results in transparent background (image or transparent if image missing). To combine: .el { background: url(image.jpg) no-repeat; background-color: red; } (order: background first, background-color after)."],
  [`display: none element — is it still in the accessibility tree and tab order?`, "med", "No. display: none removes the element from the accessibility tree AND tab order. Screen readers skip it, keyboard navigation skips it. It's as if the element doesn't exist. difference: visibility: hidden hides from screen readers too but preserves space. opacity: 0 preserves accessibility (still in tab order). For truly hidden but accessible: use clip: rect(0,0,0,0) (visually hidden, screen reader accessible)."],
  [`text-align: center on a block child inside a flex parent — does it center the block?`, "med", "No, text-align works on inline content, not block children. In a flex parent, use justify-content: center to center a block child horizontally. In a regular block parent, use margin: 0 auto on the child. text-align: center only centers inline/inline-block children, not block-level elements."],
  [`gap on flexbox — does it add space before the first or after the last item?`, "easy", "No — gap only adds space BETWEEN items, not before the first or after the last item. This is the key advantage of gap over margins — no edge spacing. If you need edge spacing, add padding on the container instead."],
  [`A 1fr grid track and a child with min-content larger than 1fr — what wins?`, "hard", "min-content wins (fr has minmax(auto, 1fr) by default). The fr unit distributes remaining space after auto-sized content. If the child has content wider than 1fr share, the track expands to fit the content. To force equality: minmax(0, 1fr) sets minimum to 0, letting the track shrink below content (content overflows or is clipped)."],
  [`calc(100% - 20px) where parent width is unknown at parse time — when is it resolved?`, "hard", "calc() is resolved at COMPUTED VALUE time (when the browser calculates final values), not parse time. The 100% is resolved relative to the parent's final computed width. So even if the parent width is determined by content or later CSS rules, calc() adapts. This is why calc() works for fluid layouts — it's evaluated during layout computation, not during style parsing."],
  [`Two selectors: ul li (specificity 0,0,2) vs .nav (0,1,0) — which wins?`, "med", ".nav wins. ul li = two element selectors (0,0,0,2), .nav = one class selector (0,0,1,0). A single class beats any number of element selectors because specificity compares left-to-right, and the class (third position) beats elements (fourth position). .nav = 0,0,1,0 vs ul li = 0,0,0,2. The 1 in the class position wins."],
];
